---
// =============================================================================
// FILE: src/pages/resume.astro
// ROUTE: /resume  (static HTML, prerendered at build time)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THIS IS
//   The public resume / CV page for Shivam Chaudhary. It is a plain, fully
//   static Astro page: all content lives in the frontmatter arrays below and is
//   rendered once at `npm run build`, then shipped to Cloudflare Pages as flat
//   HTML. There is no client-side JavaScript, no data fetching, and no
//   interactivity beyond ordinary anchor links.
//
// SINGLE RESPONSIBILITY
//   Present the author's experience, skills, publications, and education, plus
//   two outbound actions: contact links and a PDF download. Nothing else.
//
// HOW IT FITS THE ARCHITECTURE
//   - This is one of the "flat" marketing/portfolio pages. Unlike the blog,
//     /gated, auth, or payment routes, it touches NONE of the dynamic backend:
//     no Cloudflare Pages Functions, no Supabase / PostgREST, no RLS, no Resend,
//     no Turnstile, no Dodo Payments, no Supabase auth client. If you are here
//     hunting for a security bug, there is no server logic, no token check, no
//     entitlement gate, and no user input on this page — it cannot leak data
//     because it never reads any.
//   - It composes the shared <BaseLayout>, which supplies the <html>/<head>,
//     global styles, nav/footer, SEO/OG tags, etc. This page only provides the
//     <title>, <description>, and the main body content.
//
// DEPENDENCIES (imports / relies on)
//   - ../layouts/BaseLayout.astro  — page shell (head, nav, footer, global CSS
//     variables such as `--muted` used by the scoped <style> below).
//   - /resume.pdf  — a STATIC asset served from the `public/` directory (the
//     "Download PDF" link). It is not generated by this page; if the PDF is
//     stale, update the file in public/, not this component. A broken/missing
//     public/resume.pdf yields a 404 on click — nothing here validates it.
//
// WHAT DEPENDS ON THIS
//   - The site nav / any internal link pointing at `/resume`.
//   - SEO/sitemap: @astrojs/sitemap will include this route automatically.
//   - The <title>/<description> props feed OG image + meta generation via
//     BaseLayout; changing them changes the page's search/social preview.
//
// SECURITY / GOTCHAS
//   - The mailto: and profile links are hardcoded and public by design (this is
//     a resume). No PII beyond what the author intends to publish appears here.
//   - Content is authored as trusted literals below and rendered through JSX
//     expressions, so Astro HTML-escapes it — there is no `set:html` and no
//     user-supplied/untrusted input, so no XSS surface.
//   - Editing content = edit the arrays below; the template is generic and
//     should rarely need to change.
// =============================================================================

import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CONTENT MODEL
// The page is data-driven: each section maps over one of these plain arrays.
// Keeping copy as structured data (rather than inline markup) means adding a
// job / skill / publication is a one-line data edit, and the visual template
// stays untouched. Order in each array === render order on the page.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Work history, newest-first. Each entry: { role, company, dates, points[] }.
// `dates` is a free-form display string (not parsed), so any human-readable
// range works. `points` are the bullet lines rendered under the entry.
const experience = [
  {
    role: 'Sr. Data Scientist',
    company: 'Aviso AI',
    dates: "May '25 – Present",
    points: [
      'Architected a multi-agent LLM system processing 100K+ deals daily across 15 enterprise clients, replacing rule-based logic with insight generation from CRM and engagement data, governed by evaluation and safety guardrails.',
      'Replaced correlation-based explainability with a causal-inference backbone (Double Machine Learning, Causal Forests) to surface actionable recommendations — driving a ~12% increase in sales-rep engagement.',
      'Built and scaled the LLM engineering function: defined the technical hiring bar and grew the team from 1 to 5 engineers for end-to-end development and production deployment.',
    ],
  },
  {
    role: 'Sr. Computer Vision Engineer',
    company: 'Bosch Global Software Technologies',
    dates: "Aug '22 – Apr '25",
    points: [
      'Published a research paper and filed a patent on enhancing foundation models (EVA-02), leveraging 90%+ unlabeled Bosch data to boost performance by ~5%; collaborated with Bosch Romania to further improve EVA by ~4.31% via cluster analysis and hypothesis testing on DINOv2 embeddings.',
      'Built a Vision Assignment Marketplace, training SWin, DeiT, and DeTr with ~6.72% average performance deviation; designed a ConvNeXt segmentation pipeline on fisheye images (~57% F1).',
      'Deployed an object detector and motion analyzer on a Jetson Nano edge test-bed at ~30 FPS; refactored legacy code for newer vision-transformer architectures, improving adaptability/efficiency by 14%.',
    ],
  },
  {
    role: 'Data Scientist II',
    company: 'HiLabs Inc.',
    dates: "Oct '20 – Jul '22",
    points: [
      'Engineered a multi-million-dollar data pipeline moving anomalies from large-scale SQL/RDBMS sources to HBase and Solr for ticket-based remediation, improving Data Quality Index from ~78 to ~86; projected to prevent $200M+ in annual losses for a leading U.S. health insurer.',
      'Deployed an enterprise-grade OCR solution (NLP + custom algorithms) on contracts after GAN-based image enhancement, generating $100K+ in business value.',
    ],
  },
  {
    role: 'Machine Learning Engineer Intern',
    company: 'GMO Research (Japan)',
    dates: "Jan '20 – Mar '20",
    points: [
      'Forecast 6-hour survey response likelihood with time-aware Random Forests, Decision Trees, and DNNs; addressed class imbalance via SMOTE and custom metrics, achieving 91% efficacy on responsive marketing panels.',
    ],
  },
];

// Skills, grouped by category. `items` is a pre-joined display string (not an
// array) because the template prints it verbatim after the bold group label.
const skills = [
  { group: 'Languages & Libraries', items: 'Python, SQL, Scala, Pandas, NumPy, Scikit-Learn' },
  { group: 'Big Data & MLOps', items: 'Spark, Hadoop, HBase, Solr, AWS, Docker, Git, CI/CD, GPU/HPC' },
  { group: 'ML / DL / LLM', items: 'PyTorch, TensorFlow, Transformers, LangChain, LangGraph, RAG, Agentic AI, MCP' },
  { group: 'Models & Paradigms', items: 'Time-Series Forecasting, Recommendation, Causal Inference, Anomaly Detection, Computer Vision' },
];

// Publications & patents, as flat display strings (no links/DOIs modeled).
const publications = [
  'Patent (co-authored): dimensionality-reduction techniques to detect and localize failure regions in segmentation outputs from foundation models, improving interpretability and reliability.',
  'Research: a two-stage fine-tuning approach improving foundation-model segmentation performance by 5%+ mIoU using unlabeled data (semi-supervised learning).',
];
---

{/*
  BaseLayout supplies the full page chrome (head/meta/OG, nav, footer, global
  CSS). `title` and `description` flow into SEO + social-preview generation, so
  treat them as user-facing metadata, not just a browser-tab label.
*/}
<BaseLayout
  title="Resume — Shivam Chaudhary"
  description="Senior Data Scientist — production LLM/Agentic AI, ML/DL, causal inference, and computer vision."
>
  <div class="resume">
    <header class="resume-head">
      <h1>Shivam Chaudhary</h1>
      <p class="contact">
        <a href="mailto:shivam.iitmandi@gmail.com">shivam.iitmandi@gmail.com</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>
        <a href="https://github.com/shivam7569">GitHub</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/shivam7569/">LinkedIn</a>
      </p>
      {/*
        The " · " separators are decorative only, so they carry aria-hidden to
        keep screen readers from announcing stray dots between links.
        The `download` attribute hints the browser to save rather than navigate;
        /resume.pdf is a static file in public/ (see file header) — this page
        does not generate or verify it.
      */}
      <p><a class="cta" href="/resume.pdf" download>Download PDF &darr;</a></p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>Summary</h2>
      <p>
        Senior Data Scientist with 6+ years applying scientific methods to high-stakes business
        problems, building production-grade ML/DL and end-to-end LLM/Agentic AI systems at scale.
        Hands-on across time-series forecasting, causal inference, recommendation, and large-scale
        data, with a product-oriented mindset and a habit of turning research into real-world impact.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Experience</h2>
      {/* One .entry per job, in array order (newest-first). Nested map renders
          each bullet. No `key` needed — Astro renders this once at build time,
          it is not a hydrated/reconciled client component. */}
      {experience.map((job) => (
        <div class="entry">
          <div class="entry-head">
            <strong>{job.role} — {job.company}</strong>
            <span class="dates">{job.dates}</span>
          </div>
          <ul>
            {job.points.map((p) => <li>{p}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
      ))}
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Skills</h2>
      {/* `.skills` is an unbulleted list (see scoped style); the bold group
          label substitutes for a bullet. `s.items` is printed as-is. */}
      <ul class="skills">
        {skills.map((s) => (
          <li><strong>{s.group}:</strong> {s.items}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Publications & Patents</h2>
      <ul>
        {publications.map((p) => <li>{p}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </section>

    {/* Education is a single fixed entry, so it is hardcoded rather than
        data-driven like the sections above. */}
    <section>
      <h2>Education</h2>
      <div class="entry">
        <div class="entry-head">
          <strong>Indian Institute of Technology, Mandi</strong>
          <span class="dates">Aug '16 – Jul '20</span>
        </div>
        <p>B.Tech in Mechanical Engineering, Minor in Intelligent Systems.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

{/*
  Astro scopes this <style> to this component only (class names are hashed at
  build), so these rules cannot leak into other pages. `var(--muted)` is a
  global custom property defined by BaseLayout's stylesheet — this file only
  consumes it. `.entry-head` uses flex with wrap + space-between so the role
  and the right-aligned date sit on one line on wide screens and stack cleanly
  on narrow ones; `.dates` keeps `white-space: nowrap` so a date range never
  breaks mid-string.
*/}
<style>
  .resume-head { margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .resume-head h1 { margin-bottom: 0.25rem; }
  .contact { color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }
  .entry { margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
  .entry-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.25rem 1rem;
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .dates { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; white-space: nowrap; }
  .resume ul { margin: 0.4rem 0 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  .resume li { margin-bottom: 0.35rem; }
  .skills { list-style: none; padding-left: 0; }
  .skills li { margin-bottom: 0.4rem; }
</style>
